[size=5][b]Crimson Desert Telemetry[/b][/size]
[b]Live lights all around the player—including behind the camera—with light occlusion checks on by default.[/b]

Telemetry reads the game's current light data [b]before the renderer's view-dependent filtering[/b]. Turning away no longer removes those sources from the radar or telemetry stream. The game's physics queries estimate whether geometry blocks the path from the camera to each nearby light.

[b]Supports Crimson Desert patch 2.03.02 on Steam for Windows.[/b]

[size=4][b]What's new in 2.2.4[/b][/size]
[list]
[*][b]Camera-local sky visibility fixed:[/b] camera sky visibility and the derived local ambient estimate no longer stay unavailable for a whole session. Their GPU copy could start during loading, before Telemetry's submission hook existed. It now starts only once that hook is active.[/*]
[*][b]More robust sky readback:[/b] a copy that is not submitted or not completed within 5 seconds is discarded and retried. A removed graphics device reports the field as unavailable instead of a sample.[/*]
[*][b]No extra Direct3D 12 queue at world entry:[/b] Telemetry now finds the game's command submission function through the game's own presentation queue. It no longer creates its own queue while the world loads, a step that coincided with a rare GPU hang. With HUD, markers and notices all disabled, the previous method remains as fallback.[/*]
[*][b]Corrected texts:[/b] the INI and README now describe F11 correctly: it shows only lights with a fresh clear result.[/*]
[/list]
Six test starts with and without ReShade were clean. That does not prove the rare world-entry hang is gone. If the game hangs while entering the world, please report it with the four CrimsonDesertTelemetry*.log files beside the game EXE.

[size=4][b]Also included[/b][/size]
[list]
[*][b]Clear-only light stream (2.2.3):[/b] HTTP and WebSocket endpoints provide grouped, smoothed light data containing only contributions measured as clear.[/*]
[*][b]Fail-closed HUD filtering (2.2.3):[/b] with light hiding enabled, blocked, unknown, missing and stale results stay out of the markers and radar. A moving light is not briefly shown merely because its next physics result is pending.[/*]
[*][b]Raw data preserved:[/b] the original and unfiltered smoothed streams still contain all captured contributions. Unknown remains unknown in their metadata; it is not falsely reported as blocked.[/*]
[*][b]All-around light capture:[/b] current engine lights remain available beyond the camera's view, including behind you.[/*]
[*][b]Light occlusion checks:[/b] nine physics rays sample the path from the camera toward each nearby light. Results are clear, blocked or unknown. Enabled by default.[/*]
[*][b]Decoded fire groups:[/b] particle groups become summed light contributions instead of dozens of separate particle records. One physical fire can still produce several contributions.[/*]
[*][b]3D radar and fullscreen markers:[/b] filled dots indicate renderer-selected lights; hollow rings indicate current sources the renderer did not select for this view. Hollow does not mean OFF.[/*]
[*][b]ReShade 6.8 compatibility:[/b] native capture recognizes the same D3D12 device through ReShade's proxy while still rejecting a different device.[/*]
[*][b]Optional lightshow bridge:[/b] a separate companion can temporarily modulate ManyLights RGB through a local bridge that disengages when its heartbeat expires. The companion is not included in this ZIP.[/*]
[/list]

[size=4][b]What you get[/b][/size]
[list]
[*][b]Light data:[/b] world position, linear HDR RGB, derived luminance, and direction/cone angle for recognized spotlight contributions.[/*]
[*][b]Three ways to consume lights:[/b] complete raw contributions, a separate grouped and smoothed feed, or the clear-only grouped and smoothed feed.[/*]
[*][b]Player and camera:[/b] player position and root orientation, plus native render-camera position, orientation and projection parameters.[/*]
[*][b]Ambient/sky:[/b] global sky RGB, plus camera-local sky visibility and a derived local ambient estimate whenever a valid sample exists. These local fields are optional and can be null.[/*]
[*][b]Two in-game views:[/b] fullscreen light markers with detail cards and a player-centered 3D radar with the camera frustum.[/*]
[*][b]Local HTTP/WebSocket APIs:[/b] use the data in overlays, tools, research or external lighting integrations.[/*]
[*][b]Single ASI:[/b] automatic host startup, configurable hotkeys, status notices and SDR/HDR10/scRGB UI.[/*]
[/list]

[url=https://youtu.be/eyRkkTXAU64]Watch the telemetry and HUD demonstration[/url]
The video shows an earlier version; all-around capture and light occlusion were introduced in 2.2.0.
[img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ZappendusterFX/CrimsonDesertTelemetry/v2.2.4/media/screenshot1.jpg[/img]

[size=4][b]How light occlusion works[/b][/size]
The mod casts nine physics rays from the [b]camera position[/b] toward points around each nearby light, including sources behind the camera.
[list]
[*][b]Clear:[/b] at least one sampled path is unobstructed.[/*]
[*][b]Blocked:[/b] all nine sampled paths are obstructed.[/*]
[*][b]Unknown:[/b] the measurement is missing, invalid, incomplete or older than 500 ms.[/*]
[/list]
This helps distinguish a wall from a lantern's metal cage: a cage bar can block the center ray while neighboring rays still pass through.

By default, blocked markers are dimmed. Press [b]F11[/b] to show [b]only lights measured as clear[/b] in both the fullscreen markers and radar. Blocked and unknown lights are hidden in this filtered view. Press F11 again to return to the complete view.

[b]This is a sampled collision estimate, not optical transmission or a percentage of visible brightness.[/b] Narrow gaps can produce clear paths, while geometry without suitable collision may not block the queries. A newly moved light may briefly be unknown while its next measurement is pending.

The supplied radius is [b]100 game units around the player[/b], configurable from 1 to 500 through LightOverlay/Radius. Rays still originate at the camera. Up to 256 targets share a bounded query budget; crowded scenes can refresh individual lights less frequently.

The [b]raw and existing smoothed API streams remain complete[/b]: visibility metadata does not remove their light records or change their RGB. For applications that want only clear contributions, use:
[code]
GET /v1/lights/visible
WS  /v1/lights/visible/stream[/code]
This separate feed filters contributions [b]before[/b] grouping and smoothing, so blocked or unknown RGB does not leak into a visible group's color. If visibility data is unavailable, the feed reports unavailable rather than pretending the scene contains no lights.

To disable light occlusion checks:
[code]
[SourceVisibility]
Enabled=0[/code]
The raw light data remains available; the clear-only feed then cannot provide measured visible lights.

[size=4][b]Install or upgrade[/b][/size]
Requires:
[list]
[*]Windows x64 and Crimson Desert patch [b]2.03.02[/b] on Steam.[/*]
[*]Microsoft [b].NET 8 ASP.NET Core Runtime (x64)[/b].[/*]
[*]One compatible x64 ASI loader installed for the game.[/*]
[/list]
[list]
[*]Close Crimson Desert before installing, updating or removing Telemetry.[/*]
[*]Back up your INI preferences. Disable/remove the previous Telemetry package and any separate legacy console ASI.[/*]
[*]Import and [b]activate/deploy[/b] the complete CrimsonDesertTelemetry-v2.2.4-ModManagers.zip in your mod manager. Importing alone is insufficient.[/*]
[*]Keep all six runtime files together beside the game EXE. Do not mix versions.[/*]
[*]Merge your preferences into the new INI, launch the game and load a save.[/*]
[/list]
[code]
CrimsonDesertTelemetry.asi
CrimsonDesertTelemetry.Core.dll
CrimsonDesertTelemetry.ini
crimson-desert-telemetry.dll
crimson-desert-telemetry.deps.cfg
crimson-desert-telemetry.runtimeconfig.cfg[/code]

[b]DMM upgrade note:[/b] Local tests with DMM 2.8.1 and 3.1.1 found that removal/upgrades can leave old Telemetry companions behind. With the game closed and the old package removed, check these four files before redeploying. Remove only stale Telemetry copies, not your ASI loader:
[code]
CrimsonDesertTelemetry.Core.dll
crimson-desert-telemetry.dll
crimson-desert-telemetry.deps.cfg
crimson-desert-telemetry.runtimeconfig.cfg[/code]
Keep the .cfg filenames unchanged. Renaming them to .json can make DMM interpret them as game patches.

Supplied defaults:
[code]
[Lights]
Upstream=1
[SourceVisibility]
Enabled=1
[LightOverlay]
Radius=100
HideOccluded=0[/code]
Restart the game after INI changes. Hot-unloading is unsupported.

[size=4][b]Controls[/b][/size]
[list]
[*][b]F8:[/b] show/hide the corner HUD and 3D radar.[/*]
[*][b]F9:[/b] show/hide detailed diagnostics.[/*]
[*][b]F10:[/b] show/hide fullscreen light markers.[/*]
[*][b]F11:[/b] toggle between all captured lights and clear-only lights in both in-game views.[/*]
[/list]
